## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design scalable I/O budgeting, cross-CPU coordination, and NUMA-aware fairness under heavy multi-tenant workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Per-VM isolation and quotas
- NUMA-aware accounting
- Token-bucket throttling
- Cross-CPU coordination via IPI
- CAS-based atomic updates

## Code Example

```c
struct vm_budget {
    atomic_long budget_bytes;
    atomic_long tokens;
    int numa_node;
};

void refill(struct vm_budget *vb) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&vb->tokens, REFILL_TOKEN);
}

static bool try_consume(struct vm_budget *vb, long cost) {
    long old, new;
    do {
        old = atomic_load(&vb->tokens);
        if (old < cost)
            return false;
        new = old - cost;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&vb->tokens, &old, new));
    return true;
}

void submit_io(struct vm_budget *vb, struct bio *bio) {
    if (!try_consume(vb, bio->size))
        enqueue_throttled(vb->numa_node, bio);
    else
        dispatch_io(bio);
}
```

